\title{The Price of Universality is a Submodular Set Function}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a class $\mathcal S$ of probabilistic sources on a finite message set $X$, the minimax pointwise regret of universal coding equals $\log_2 C_{\mathcal S}$ bits, where $C_{\mathcal S} = \sum_x \sup_\theta p_\theta(x)$ is the Shtarkov sum --- the $\ell^1$ norm of the maximum-likelihood envelope. We determine exactly how this \emph{price of universality} responds to enlarging the class: adjoining a source $p$ changes the Shtarkov sum by $\sum_x (p(x) - \hat p_{\mathcal S}(x))^{+}$, the mass on which the newcomer strictly outbids the incumbent envelope. The increment vanishes precisely when $p$ is pointwise dominated, so mixtures of class members are free and only extreme points matter. Since the positive part is antitone in the incumbent level, the formula yields diminishing returns. Making this precise, we prove that the library price functional $C(A) = \sum_x \max_{i \in A} P_i(x)$ attached to a pool of candidate models is a monotone submodular set function vanishing on the empty library; that it is even multiplicatively submodular, $C(A \cup B) C(A \cap B) \le C(A) C(B)$, whence the price in bits is submodular under a positivity guard that a two-point-mass example shows to be necessary; and that consequently greedy library design is $(1 - 1/e)$-optimal, i.e.\ within $\log_2 \frac{e}{e-1} < 0.67$ bits of the best library of the same size. We also record the exact identity $C(\{p,q\}) = 1 + \|p-q\|_{\TV}$ and a worked four-model pool with exact prices.
\end{abstract}

