\end{center}

Exact prices: $C(\{P_i\}) = 1$ for every $i$; $C(\{P_0,P_1\}) = \tfrac54$; $C(\{P_0,P_3\}) = \tfrac74$; $C(\{P_0,P_1,P_2\}) = \tfrac43$; $C(\{P_0,P_1,P_3\}) = 2$.

A single model is free ($0$ bits). Similar models are cheap: $\|P_0-P_1\|_{\TV} = \tfrac14$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} predicts $\tfrac54$, as observed. Eccentric models are valuable and sensible ones need not be: the marginal value of $P_3$ over $\{P_0\}$ is $(0-\tfrac12)^{+}+(0-\tfrac14)^{+}+(1-\tfrac14)^{+} = \tfrac34$, three times that of $P_1$, whereas the uniform model $P_2$ exceeds the envelope $(\tfrac12,\tfrac12,\tfrac14)$ of $\{P_0,P_1\}$ only on $a_2$ and only by $\tfrac1{12}$; consequently the three-model library $\{P_0,P_1,P_2\}$ with $C = \tfrac43$ is worth \emph{less} than the two-model library $\{P_0,P_3\}$ with $C = \tfrac74$.

Greedy is exact here: from $\emptyset$ all singletons tie at gain $1$; taking $P_0$, the gains are $\tfrac14$ for $P_1$, $\tfrac16$ for $P_2$ and $\tfrac34$ for $P_3$, so $P_3$ enters, giving $C = \tfrac74$; then $P_1$ (gain $\tfrac14$) beats $P_2$ (gain $\tfrac1{12}$), giving $C = 2$, which is the maximum over all three-element libraries --- comfortably above the guaranteed $0.632 \times 2 = 1.264$.

Exhaustive computation on an integer-scaled copy of this pool (all probabilities multiplied by $12$, which scales $C$ by $12$ and preserves every inequality) confirms Theorem~\ref{thm:libmarg} for all $16 \times 4$ pairs (library, inserted model), Theorem~\ref{thm:submod} for all $16 \times 16$ pairs of libraries, and Theorem~\ref{thm:dim} for all nested pairs, in exact integer arithmetic.

\section{Applications}

\emph{Codec and decompressor-library design.} An archiver shipping $n$ built-in models is solving exactly the cardinality-constrained maximisation of $C$; greedy selection by marginal value lands within $0.67$ bits of the best possible library.

\emph{Deduplication of model zoos.} A model may be deleted without loss of reach precisely when it is pointwise dominated by the envelope of the others; and every mixture of retained models is automatically redundant, so a library closed under mixture prunes to its extreme points.

\emph{Model selection and MDL.} The parametric complexity of a model class in the minimum description length framework is $\log_2 C_{\mathcal S}$; the marginal value formula prices the complexity cost of enriching a family without recomputing the normalising constant.

\emph{Diversity measurement.} $C(A) - 1$ is a submodular multi-way generalisation of total variation, so diversity budgets can be optimised greedily with guarantees.

\emph{Cryptographic and steganographic covers.} Where a cover distribution must plausibly explain traffic from several sources at once, $C$ quantifies the unavoidable statistical slack, and the marginal value formula says which extra traffic model genuinely widens the cover.

\section{Discussion}

The technical core is one line, $\max(a,b) - b = (a-b)^{+}$, applied to a quantity that happens to be an $\ell^1$ norm of a pointwise supremum. The Shtarkov sum is usually studied asymptotically, and asymptotics hide exact combinatorial structure; read non-asymptotically, it is a \emph{coverage functional}, a max-of-sums whose increments are positive parts.

Three contrasts deserve emphasis. First, the results are exact and assumption-free: no smoothness, no parametric structure, no asymptotics; the parameter set may be infinite and suprema need not be attained. Second, the bit-level statement needs care at the boundary, where the generic concave-composition route fails because $\log_2 0$ is not finite; the additive inequality plus monotonicity upgrades instead to the multiplicative one, which passes through the logarithm wherever it is defined, and the two-point-mass example marks the exact edge of validity. Third, the guarantee is worst-case and often pessimistic: on the worked pool greedy is optimal.

A limitation: everything concerns a fixed finite message set, i.e.\ a fixed block length. The library calculus applies verbatim at each length, but whether greedy libraries are \emph{nested} across block lengths is not addressed and appears subtle.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Matroid constraints and continuous greedy]
Under a matroid constraint (e.g.\ ``at most $k$ models per data modality'', a partition matroid) plain greedy is only $1/2$-optimal, but the multilinear extension
$F(y) = \mathbb E_{A\sim y} C(A) = \sum_x \mathbb E_{A \sim y} \max_{i\in A}P_i(x)$
is concave along nonnegative directions and has gradient
$\partial F/\partial y_j = \sum_x \mathbb E_{A\sim y}(P_j(x) - \env_A(x))^{+}$,
so continuous greedy restores the factor $1-1/e$. The point is that this is an explicit $\ell^1$ norm of an expectation of positive parts rather than an abstract oracle expectation: the discrete derivative is already in closed form and diminishing returns is exactly the sign condition needed for the cross-derivatives.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Curvature: better than $1-1/e$ for smooth pools]
Define the curvature of a pool $\Omega$ by $\kappa = 1 - \min_{j\in\Omega} \frac{C(\Omega)-C(\Omega\setminus\{j\})}{C(\{j\})}$. Then greedy achieves the factor $(1-e^{-\kappa})/\kappa$, and for pools whose members are pairwise within total variation $\delta$ one has $\kappa \le \delta|\Omega|$, so nearly identical pools admit nearly optimal greedy libraries. Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} identifies total variation as the right modulus for controlling curvature.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Hardness of exact design]
Deciding, for a rational pool and thresholds $k, t$, whether some library of size $k$ has $C(A) \ge t$ is NP-hard --- plausibly by reduction from Max Coverage, whose coverage functional is the special case in which every $P_i$ is a normalised indicator.
\end{conjecture}

Extending the calculus from a fixed alphabet to sequences, and to \emph{adaptive} libraries whose members are chosen online as data arrives, would connect the marginal value formula to the switching-and-mixing literature of online learning, where the same envelope appears as the best-expert-in-hindsight benchmark.

\section{Summary of main results}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Marginal value formula:} $C_{\mathcal S\cup\{p\}} = C_{\mathcal S} + \sum_x (p(x)-\hat p_{\mathcal S}(x))^{+}$, with $\hat p_{\mathcal T}$ in place of $p$ for merging whole classes.
\item \textbf{Dichotomy:} zero increment iff pointwise domination, strict increase otherwise; mixtures of class members are free.
\item \textbf{Bounds:} the increment is at most $\sum_x|p(x)-\hat p_{\mathcal S}(x)|$ and at most $1$.
\item \textbf{Library functional:} monotone, vanishing on $\emptyset$, with closed-form marginals, submodular, with diminishing returns and the covering inequality.
\item \textbf{Bridge:} for a nonempty library of genuine sources, $C(A)$ is the Shtarkov sum of the corresponding class, and $C(A) \ge 1$.
\item \textbf{Bit-level submodularity:} $C(A\cup B)C(A\cap B) \le C(A)C(B)$, hence $\log_2 C$ is submodular when $C(A\cap B) > 0$; the guard is necessary.
\item \textbf{Two-model identity:} $C(\{p,q\}) = 1 + \|p-q\|_{\TV}$.
\item \textbf{Greedy design:} $C(B)-C(A_k) \le (1-1/|B|)^k C(B)$, hence $C(A_n) \ge (1-e^{-1})C(B)$ and greedy is within $\log_2\frac{e}{e-1} < 0.67$ bits of the best library of the same size.
\end{enumerate}

